🔓 100% open source · social network, photos and files on your own device
A social network your family owns.Off the cloud, at home.
@img.src=/img/hero-1600.jpg
@img.alt=An Off The Cloud device on a desk, next to a camera and a computer showing your photo library
Off The Cloud is a private social network for your family and friends, and a home for your photos and files, on a small device you own. Post photos and videos, comment and like, with no ads, no algorithm and no strangers: your posts stay on your device and go only to the devices of friends you accept, never stored on a company's servers. The same device backs up everyone's phones in full resolution and keeps your computers' folders in sync, so it takes the place of Instagram, Google Photos, iCloud, Google Drive and Dropbox at once. It's free, open-source software for a Raspberry Pi 5, set up from your phone.
@a.href=https://demo.off-the.cloud/
Try the demo Look around a real device with a made-up family's photos and files. No sign-up.
Get your device running
Computer app Recommended
Raspberry Pi 5 image
Install script
The easiest way: the app downloads the image and checks its signature, writes the card itself on Windows and Linux, and guides you through each step.
For a Raspberry Pi 5 with at least 8 GB of RAM, a MicroSD card for the system and, optionally, two USB disks for RAID-1, plugged into the Pi's two blue USB ports. Use the official Raspberry Pi 27 W USB-C power supply (or a better one): with a weaker one the Pi can restart from low voltage while it processes your photos. No keyboard, monitor or Linux knowledge needed. Only have a 4 GB Pi? It can run, but only with Image Tagging and Face Recognition turned off in Settings, before you back up your photos: those models need more memory than 4 GB leaves.
@a.href=https://apps.apple.com/app/id6814658734
@a.download
@a.href=https://github.com/alonsovidales/otc/releases/download/desktop/otc-sync-windows-amd64.exe
@a.download
@a.href=https://github.com/alonsovidales/otc/releases/download/desktop/otc-sync-linux-amd64
Get it on the Mac App Store Download for Windows Download for Linux
@a.href=#download
ARM versions, and first-run tips for Windows and Linux, are in the download section.
Install the app and choose Set Up a New Device…: in the Mac app's Settings, or in the tray menu on Windows and Linux.
@a.href=https://www.raspberrypi.com/software/
Make the card. The app downloads the image and checks it against the project's signature. On Windows and Linux it then writes it to a MicroSD card of 8 GB or more, after asking for administrator permission; on a Mac it opens Raspberry Pi Imager to write the card and tells you what to choose there.
Plug in the disks (into the two blue USB ports), insert the card and power the Pi on. After a minute it announces itself over Bluetooth.
Finish the setup over Bluetooth. On a Mac, right in the app: On this Mac (or use your phone). On Windows and Linux, in the phone app (iOS or Android): tap Set up a new device. Either way it asks for your WiFi, a name and password for the device and how to use the disks, then installs the software - about 20 minutes.
Sign in when it's done: the app connects to the new device by itself with the password you chose, or open https://<name>.off-the.cloud in a browser.
For a Raspberry Pi 5 with at least 8 GB of RAM, a MicroSD card for the system and, optionally, two USB disks for RAID-1, plugged into the Pi's two blue USB ports. Use the official Raspberry Pi 27 W USB-C power supply (or a better one): with a weaker one the Pi can restart from low voltage while it processes your photos. No keyboard, monitor or Linux knowledge needed. Only have a 4 GB Pi? It can run, but only with Image Tagging and Face Recognition turned off in Settings, before you back up your photos: those models need more memory than 4 GB leaves.
@a.href=https://github.com/alonsovidales/otc/releases/download/image/off-the-cloud-rpi-lite-arm64.img.xz
⬇ Download the image (≈ 530 MB)
@a.href=https://www.raspberrypi.com/software/
Flash it to the MicroSD card with Raspberry Pi Imager: "Use custom", pick the file, write. Skip Imager's own customisation step.
Plug in the disks (into the two blue USB ports), insert the card and power the Pi on. After a minute it announces itself over Bluetooth.
Set it up from your phone with the app (iOS or Android): on the welcome screen tap Set up a new device. It finds the Pi over Bluetooth and runs the setup right there - your phone stays on its own WiFi. It asks for your WiFi, a name and password for the device and how to use the disks, then installs the software - about 20 minutes.
Sign in when it's done: the app connects to the new device by itself with the password you chose, or open https://<name>.off-the.cloud in a browser.
@a.href=https://github.com/alonsovidales/otc/releases/download/image/off-the-cloud-rpi-lite-arm64.img.xz.sha256
The image only carries the setup wizard: it installs the current release, so it never goes out of date. Checksum: .sha256.
One command on any machine you can already log into: Ubuntu, Debian, Raspberry Pi OS and other Debian-based distros (64-bit).
Terminal
Copy
# log into the machine, then: curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/alonsovidales/otc/main/scripts/verified-install.sh \ | sudo bash -s -- <subdomain>
@a.href=https://github.com/alonsovidales/otc#readme
It installs everything and builds the software from source, then starts the service at http://<device-ip>:8080/. Safe to re-run to update. Your files go on two disks mirrored as RAID-1: it reuses a mirror it finds and only erases two new disks to build one when you confirm it, and it installs the status lights too. A single disk works as well (see the README). The WiFi setup isn't part of this path — see the image for a from-scratch Raspberry Pi.
@a.href=/account?signup=1
Want to reach it from outside your home network? Create an account for the bridge →
Off The Cloud at a glance
What it is
A private social network for family and friends, and a home server (NAS) for your photos, videos and files, with its own apps
Social network
Posts with photos and videos, comments, likes and notifications, only for the friends you accept, from device to device; newest first, no ads
Files
A NAS: folders kept in sync with Mac, Windows and Linux computers or backed up, upload-only folders that keep earlier versions, and share links
Instead of
Instagram, Facebook or a family WhatsApp group for sharing photos; Google Photos or iCloud Photos for backup; Google Drive, Dropbox, iCloud Drive or Proton Drive for files
Runs on
A Raspberry Pi 5 with 8 GB (4 GB with tagging and faces off), or a 64-bit Intel/AMD or ARM machine with Debian or Ubuntu
Storage
Two USB drives mirrored as RAID-1, each with a full copy: sticks, SSDs, hard disks or microSD cards in USB readers
Apps
iPhone and iPad, Android, Mac, Windows and Linux, plus a web app in your browser
Cost
Free software; a Raspberry Pi 5 and two USB pen drives; optional bridge: free for two years, then €9.99 + VAT a year
Privacy
File contents encrypted at rest; photo tagging and faces done on the device; nothing used for ads or AI training
Licence
Open source under the GNU AGPL-3.0: the device, every app and the bridge
Languages
English, Spanish, French, German, Italian, Portuguese and Dutch
