FAQ
Questions people actually ask
How does the private social network work?
Everyone in it has an address of their own, like casa.off-the.cloud, and you add friends by their device's name. Once they accept, you see each other's posts in Social, in the phone apps and the web app: photos and videos with a caption, comments and likes, newest first, and a notification when someone likes or comments. Nothing is suggested or promoted, and there are no ads.
Your posts are kept on your own device, and your friends' devices fetch them from it, so there's no central server holding everyone's posts. Devices reach each other through the bridge, which relays the connection without keeping anything that passes through. If you stop being friends, you can remove everything they shared from your device, and ask their device to delete what you shared there.
There are no private messages or chats, on purpose: it leaves less room for bullying or for a stranger to approach a child. It's a feed for sharing photos and videos with the people you choose.
Do my friends and family need a device of their own?
Not necessarily. On your device you can add an account for each family member, or for a close friend, with their own photo backup, library and password. With an off-the.cloud address of its own, an account works from anywhere in the same apps and can be your friend in the feed. We recommend no more than five accounts on one device (see the question about family accounts below).
Friends who want their photos and files at home too set up a device of their own: the software is the same, and free. Everyone in the feed needs an off-the.cloud address, which comes with the bridge (see the question about the bridge below), unless you all use a bridge of your own. For people with no account at all, send a collection as a link: they open it in their browser, with no app.
How is it different from Instagram or Facebook?
Instagram and Facebook are free because advertising pays for them: your posts are kept on Meta's servers, an algorithm ranks what you see, and Meta uses public posts and comments to train its AI unless you object. On Off The Cloud your posts are on your own device, only the friends you accept see them, the feed is simply newest first, and nothing is used for ads or to train AI.
It's also made for a smaller circle: not for building an audience or following strangers, but for the family and friends you'd show your photos to at home.
@a.href=/private-social-network
How it compares with Instagram and Facebook
Can it replace Google Drive, Dropbox or Proton Drive?
For your own files, yes. Files, in the web app and the phone apps, works like a drive: folders, uploads and downloads, moving and renaming, and share links that expire. The Mac, Windows and Linux apps keep chosen folders in two-way sync with the device or back them up, and a folder made upload-only can't have anything deleted from it and keeps the earlier version of a file uploaded again.
You get as much space as one of your two drives holds, with no plan to upgrade: when they fill up, you swap them for bigger ones.
@a.href=/google-drive-alternative
How it compares with Google Drive and Dropbox
How is it different from Google Photos or iCloud?
Your photos live in your home instead of on a company's servers. You buy the hardware once instead of paying for a storage plan, and you have as much space as one of your two drives holds, since each keeps a full copy: when they fill up, you swap them for bigger ones.
Day to day it feels familiar. The phone apps back up your photos and videos in the background, in full resolution, and you can search them by what they show and, if you turn on face recognition, by who is in them. iPhones, Android phones and computers all work with it, with an account for each person in the family.
What changes is that you take ownership. Your photos are on two mirrored drives in your home, and a full backup is easy: take one drive out and put a new one in, and the device copies everything onto it; the drive you took out is your backup (see "How do I back up my device?"). Away from home, your apps reach it through the bridge (free for the first two years, then €9.99 + VAT a year), Tailscale Funnel or your own VPN. And there's no password reset: if you lose your device password, nobody can open your files, not even us.
@a.href=/google-photos-alternative
How it compares with Google Photos and iCloud
How is it different from Immich, PhotoPrism or Nextcloud?
Immich and PhotoPrism are photo libraries and Nextcloud is a file and collaboration suite. They're free and open source, but they're made for people who run their own server: you install them yourself, usually with Docker on a Linux machine, and work out storage, backups and remote access on your own.
Off The Cloud is easier to install and needs almost no technical knowledge, and it does more than photos:
It installs from an app: the computer app prepares the Raspberry Pi's card, and the setup finishes over Bluetooth from your phone or Mac, with no terminal.
It runs on inexpensive hardware: a Raspberry Pi 5 and two USB pen drives, mirrored, with alerts that say which one to replace.
Files encrypted at rest, with a key only your password unlocks.
It's a NAS for your files too, kept in two-way sync with your computers, not only a photo library.
A private social network between friends' devices, with no ads and no algorithm, which Immich and PhotoPrism don't have.
Apps for phones and computers, an account for each person in the family, and an optional bridge to reach it from anywhere without opening ports on your router.
@a.href=/google-photos-alternative
How it compares with Google Photos and iCloud
Do I need to buy special hardware?
A Raspberry Pi 5 (8GB) with a micro-SD card for the system and two USB drives for storage, mirrored so that each holds a full copy of your files: USB sticks, SSDs, hard disks or micro-SD cards in USB card readers all work. Plug them into the Pi's two blue USB ports (the fast ones; the device then tells you which one to replace if a drive ever fails). Add a power supply and an active cooler: all off-the-shelf. See the README for the exact recommended parts list. A Pi 5 with 4 GB can work too, with photo tagging and face recognition turned off.
Is there a pre-built image I can just flash?
@a.href=#install
Yes — for a Raspberry Pi 5 with at least 8 GB of RAM (a 4 GB one works only with Image Tagging and Face Recognition turned off in Settings): see the Raspberry Pi 5 image tab at the top. Flash it, power the Pi on and set it up from your phone: tap "Set up a new device" in the iOS or Android app, which finds the Pi over Bluetooth and walks you through the rest. For any other Debian/Ubuntu machine, use the install script.
Can I use something other than a Raspberry Pi, like an Orange Pi or an old PC?
@a.href=#install
Yes. The install script works on any 64-bit machine, Intel/AMD or ARM, running Ubuntu, Debian or another Debian-based Linux: an old PC or laptop, a mini PC, or a board such as an Orange Pi with a Debian or Ubuntu image. It builds the software on the machine itself, so you get the same app: photo backup, folder sync, photo tagging, face recognition, friends, sharing and updates from Settings. Give it 8 GB of memory, like the Raspberry Pi 5 it was designed for.
A few things only the Raspberry Pi 5 has:
The ready-made image and setup from your phone over Bluetooth. Elsewhere you run one command in a terminal, and the first password typed into the device becomes its password, so open it and choose one straight away.
Knowing which disk to replace. On a Pi the alert names the USB port the failed disk is in; elsewhere it says "one of the two storage disks" and gives the disk's system name and serial number.
The status lights, which are wired to the Pi's own pins (see below).
@a.href=/account
The script can mirror two disks on other machines too, but only when you ask it to, because it erases both. If it finds no disks to mirror, it stops and tells you how to install on a single disk. To give the machine an off-the.cloud address, add the name on your account page first and copy the identity it shows into the machine before running the script.
How do I move my photos from iCloud?
Use the iPhone app. When you sign in to your device, it asks which photos and videos to back up: choose "All photos and videos". Already signed in? Tap Sync All in Settings. "Sync from iCloud" is on from the start: when a photo's original is only in iCloud, as with "Optimise iPhone Storage", the iPhone downloads it first, then sends it to your device with its date.
A whole library can take a long time and use a lot of data, so turn on "Wi-Fi only" if you'd rather not use mobile data. Backing up only copies your photos and leaves your iCloud library as it is: once your device has everything, what you keep in iCloud is up to you.
The Mac app backs up folders, not the Photos library, so the iPhone, or an iPad on the same iCloud account, is the way to bring your library over.
How do I move my photos from Google Photos?
@a.href=#download
There's no Google Photos importer. Photos and videos still on your phone are backed up by the iPhone or Android app like any others, with their dates. For the rest, download your library with Google Takeout, unzip it on a computer and back up that folder with the Mac, Windows or Linux app: the photos and videos go to your device and appear in Images, tagged and searchable.
Two things to know. Images puts each photo at its file's date, not the date it was taken, so photos from Takeout can end up in the wrong month; More info still shows the date stored in the photo, when it has one. And the .json files Takeout adds next to your photos, with each photo's date, description and place, aren't read: they're kept as ordinary files.
Does it keep RAW files and videos in full quality?
Yes. The device keeps every file exactly as it arrives: nothing is resized, converted or recompressed, so RAW files, HEIC photos and videos come back byte for byte.
The phone apps send each photo's and video's original file. On an iPhone, that means an edit made in Photos isn't backed up, only the original, and a Live Photo is backed up as its still photo.
@a.href=#download
For a camera's RAW files, back up the folder you copy them into with the Mac, Windows or Linux app: every file goes to the device as it is. The device makes a preview of some RAW files and tags those like any other photo; the others get no preview and don't show in Images, but they're kept all the same in Files.
What you post to your friends' feed can be a lighter copy: a large video is made smaller, and a HEIC photo goes as a JPEG. The original in your library stays as it was.
How do I wire the status lights?
The lights are optional: one per storage disk, green when it's fine and red when it needs you. Each light is a red LED and a green LED side by side (or one two-colour LED with three legs), and each colour connects to one pin of the Raspberry Pi through a 330 Ω resistor. Wire them with the Pi switched off. The device lights them by itself; there's nothing to set up.
Wiring the two status lights to the Raspberry Pi 5
Pins 1 to 16 of the Raspberry Pi 5's 40-pin header, with pin 1 (3.3 volts, square) at the top and pins 17 to 40 continuing towards the USB ports. Light 1: pin 11 (GPIO17) through a 330 ohm resistor to the long leg of the red LED, and pin 13 (GPIO27) through a 330 ohm resistor to the long leg of the green LED; both short legs go to pin 9 (ground). Light 2: pin 15 (GPIO22) through a 330 ohm resistor to the red LED, and pin 16 (GPIO23) through a 330 ohm resistor to the green LED; both short legs go to pin 14 (ground). The same connections are listed below the drawing.
